#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186216949 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "61c3724b-7fda-5547-b0cb-7bf9136fc4a3" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Gravitational Force Between Two Objects"}] :question/id #uuid "f37e7ffd-df2d-51a4-9eee-e53589011f6b" :question/math-academy-id "e-21744" :question/problem "The Sun has a mass of $(1.99\\times10^{30})\\,\\textrm{kg}$ and Mars has a mass of $(6.42\\times10^{23})\\,\\textrm{kg}.$\nThe average distance between the centers of the Sun and Mars is $(2.28\\times10^{11})\\,\\textrm{m}.$ According to Newton's law of universal gravitation, what is the magnitude of the gravitational force between the Sun and Mars? Write your answer in scientific notation, rounding to **three significant figures** where appropriate. Assume the Newtonian constant of gravitation is $G = (6.67\\times10^{-11})\\,\\textrm{m}^3/(\\textrm{kg}\\cdot\\textrm{s}^2).$" :question/worked-solution "Newton's law of universal gravitation states that the gravitational force attracting two objects is proportional to the product of the two masses and inversely proportional to the square of the distance between their centers of mass:\n\n$$\nF = G\\dfrac{m_1m_2}{r^2}\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- $F$ is the gravitational force between the objects, measured in Newtons $(\\textrm{N}),$\n\n- $G = (6.67\\times10^{-11})\\,\\textrm{m}^3/(\\textrm{kg}\\cdot\\textrm{s}^2)$ is the Newtonian constant of gravitation,\n\n- $m_1$ and $m_2$ are the masses of the objects, measured in $\\textrm{kg},$ and\n\n- $r$ is the distance between their centers of mass, measured in $\\textrm{m}.$\n\nLet $m_1$ be the mass of the Sun and $m_2$ be the mass of Mars. Then, substituting in the known values, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F & =(6.67\\times {10}^{-11})\\cdot \\frac{(1.99\\times {10}^{30})\\cdot (6.42\\times {10}^{23})}{(2.28\\times {10}^{11})^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{6.67\\cdot 1.99\\cdot 6.42}{(2.28)^{2}}\\times \\frac{{10}^{-11}\\cdot {10}^{30}\\cdot {10}^{23}}{({10}^{11})^{2}} \\\\  & =(16.392\\,5…)\\times {10}^{20} \\\\  & =(1.639\\,25…)\\times {10}^{21}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, rounded to three significant figures, the gravitational force between the Sun and Mars is\n$\\Big(1.64 \\times 10^{21}\\Big) \\,\\textrm{N}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186216950 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "cd5da040-4fa3-5aeb-a98f-2a2ba03f5e16" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Mass of an Object"}] :question/id #uuid "2ef6ba7e-4f1d-51d2-af64-e1a77a7d748f" :question/math-academy-id "e-21745" :question/problem "The centers of two objects are separated by a distance of $(3.0\\times10^3)\\,\\textrm{m}$ and the magnitude of the gravitational force between them is $(3.0\\times10^{-4})\\,\\textrm{N}.$ If one object has mass $(2.0\\times10^5)\\,\\textrm{kg},$ then according to Newton's law of universal gravitation, what is the mass of the other object? Write your answer in scientific notation, rounding to **two significant figures** where appropriate. Assume the Newtonian constant of gravitation is $G = (6.67\\times10^{-11})\\,\\textrm{m}^3/(\\textrm{kg}\\cdot\\textrm{s}^2).$" :question/worked-solution "Newton's law of universal gravitation states that the gravitational force attracting two objects is proportional to the product of the two masses and inversely proportional to the square of the distance between their centers of mass:\n\n$$\nF = G\\dfrac{m_1m_2}{r^2}\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- $F$ is the gravitational force between the objects, measured in Newtons $(\\textrm{N}),$\n\n- $G = (6.67\\times10^{-11})\\,\\textrm{m}^3/(\\textrm{kg}\\cdot\\textrm{s}^2)$ is the Newtonian constant of gravitation,\n\n- $m_1$ and $m_2$ are the masses of the objects, measured in $\\textrm{kg},$ and\n\n- $r$ is the distance between their centers of mass, measured in $\\textrm{m}.$\n\nLet $m_2$ be the unknown mass.\nFirst, let's rearrange the formula to make $m_2$ the subject:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F & =G\\frac{{m}_{1}{m}_{2}}{{r}^{2}} \\\\ F{r}^{2} & =G{m}_{1}{m}_{2} \\\\ {m}_{2} & =\\frac{F{r}^{2}}{G{m}_{1}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, substituting in the known values, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{m}_{2} & =\\frac{(3.0\\times {10}^{-4})\\cdot (3.0\\times {10}^{3})^{2}}{(6.67\\times {10}^{-11})\\cdot (2.0\\times {10}^{5})} \\\\  & =\\frac{3.0\\cdot (3.0)^{2}}{6.67\\cdot 2.0}\\times \\frac{{10}^{-4}\\cdot ({10}^{3})^{2}}{{10}^{-11}\\cdot {10}^{5}} \\\\  & =(2.023…)\\times {10}^{8}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, rounded to two significant figures, the mass of the other object is\n$\\Big(2.0 \\times 10^{8}\\Big) \\,\\textrm{kg}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186216951 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "11810d4b-610c-5dac-8fe7-abf9391c2aa7" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Distance Between Two Objects"}] :question/id #uuid "e7043d87-7f25-55a3-bd36-49d4ba1ae3fb" :question/math-academy-id "e-21746" :question/problem "Earth has a mass of $(5.97\\times10^{24})\\,\\textrm{kg}$ and the Moon has a mass of $(7.35\\times10^{22})\\,\\textrm{kg}.$ The magnitude of the gravitational force between them is $(1.98\\times10^{20})\\,\\textrm{N}.$ According to Newton's law of universal gravitation, what is the distance between the centers of mass of Earth and the Moon? Write your answer in scientific notation, rounding to **three significant figures** where appropriate. Assume the Newtonian constant of gravitation is $G = (6.67\\times10^{-11})\\,\\textrm{m}^3/(\\textrm{kg}\\cdot\\textrm{s}^2).$" :question/worked-solution "Newton's law of universal gravitation states that the gravitational force attracting two objects is proportional to the product of the two masses and inversely proportional to the square of the distance between their centers of mass:\n\n$$\nF = G\\dfrac{m_1m_2}{r^2}\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- $F$ is the gravitational force between the objects, measured in Newtons $(\\textrm{N}),$\n\n- $G = (6.67\\times10^{-11})\\,\\textrm{m}^3/(\\textrm{kg}\\cdot\\textrm{s}^2)$ is the Newtonian constant of gravitation,\n\n- $m_1$ and $m_2$ are the masses of the objects, measured in $\\textrm{kg},$ and\n\n- $r$ is the distance between their centers of mass, measured in $\\textrm{m}.$\n\nFirst, let's rearrange the formula to make $r$ the subject:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F & =G\\frac{{m}_{1}{m}_{2}}{{r}^{2}} \\\\ {r}^{2} & =G\\frac{{m}_{1}{m}_{2}}{F} \\\\ r & =\\sqrt{G\\frac{{m}_{1}{m}_{2}}{F}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet $m_1$ be the mass of Earth and $m_2$ be the mass of the Moon. Then, substituting in the known values, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{2} & =(6.67\\times {10}^{-11})\\cdot \\frac{(5.97\\times {10}^{24})\\cdot (7.35\\times {10}^{22})}{1.98\\times {10}^{20}} \\\\  & =\\frac{6.67\\cdot 5.97\\cdot 7.35}{1.98}\\times \\frac{{10}^{-11}\\cdot {10}^{24}\\cdot {10}^{22}}{{10}^{20}} \\\\  & =(147.816…)\\times {10}^{15} \\\\  & =(14.7816…)\\times {10}^{16}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, taking the square root, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =\\sqrt{14.7816…\\times {10}^{16}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{14.7816…}\\times \\sqrt{{10}^{16}} \\\\  & =(3.8446…)\\times {10}^{8}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, rounded to three significant figures, the distance between Earth and the Moon is\n$\\Big(3.84 \\times 10^{8}\\Big) \\,\\textrm{m}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186325731 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "61c3724b-7fda-5547-b0cb-7bf9136fc4a3" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Gravitational Force Between Two Objects"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "347894f1-b489-58d0-96fe-e4894b4878c2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3.3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325733}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "347894f1-b489-58d0-96fe-e4894b4878c2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3.3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325733} :answer-field/id #uuid "b90c36ac-309b-59e9-ba69-0f3aa10a52fa" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325732} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e5f8e132-5234-5b68-9dfc-d7db6f7141a6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10^{-6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325735}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e5f8e132-5234-5b68-9dfc-d7db6f7141a6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10^{-6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325735} :answer-field/id #uuid "2a1e5040-179a-58fd-a1e0-ccaeab424db7" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325734}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "ec75030b-599a-5f41-bcd2-bdc4747e8a66" :question/math-academy-id "q-336273" :question/problem "The centers of two objects of masses $(3.4\\times {10}^{3})\\,\\text{kg}$ and $(9.0\\times {10}^{7})\\,\\text{kg}$ are separated by a distance of $(2.5\\times {10}^{3})\\,\\text{m}.$ According to Newton's law of universal gravitation, what is the magnitude of the gravitational force between the two objects? Write your answer in scientific notation, rounding to **two significant figures** where appropriate. Assume the Newtonian constant of gravitation is $G=(6.67\\times {10}^{-11})\\,{\\text{m}}^{3}/(\\text{kg}\\cdot {\\text{s}}^{2}).$\n\nThe magnitude of the force between the two objects is\n$(${{field-1}}\n$\\times $\n{{field-2}}$)\\,\\text{N}.$" :question/worked-solution "Newton's law of universal gravitation states that the gravitational force attracting two objects is proportional to the product of the two masses and inversely proportional to the square of the distance between their centers of mass:\n\n$$\nF=G\\frac{{m}_{1}{m}_{2}}{{r}^{2}}\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- $F$ is the gravitational force between the objects, measured in Newtons $(\\text{N}),$\n\n- $G=(6.67\\times {10}^{-11})\\,{\\text{m}}^{3}/(\\text{kg}\\cdot {\\text{s}}^{2})$ is the Newtonian constant of gravitation,\n\n- ${m}_{1}$ and ${m}_{2}$ are the masses of the objects, measured in $\\text{kg},$ and\n\n- $r$ is the distance between their centers of mass, measured in $\\text{m}.$\n\nChoosing ${m}_{1}=(3.4\\times {10}^{3})\\,\\text{kg}$ and ${m}_{2}=(9.0\\times {10}^{7})\\,\\text{kg}$ and substituting in the other known values, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F & =(6.67\\times {10}^{-11})\\cdot \\frac{(3.4\\times {10}^{3})\\cdot (9.0\\times {10}^{7})}{(2.5\\times {10}^{3})^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{6.67\\cdot 3.4\\cdot 9.0}{(2.5)^{2}}\\times \\frac{{10}^{-11}\\cdot {10}^{3}\\cdot {10}^{7}}{({10}^{3})^{2}} \\\\  & =32.656\\,32\\times {10}^{-7} \\\\  & =3.265\\,632\\times {10}^{-6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, rounded to two significant figures, the magnitude of the gravitational force between the two objects is\n$(\\,3.3\\times {10}^{-6}\\,)\\,\\text{N}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186325736 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "61c3724b-7fda-5547-b0cb-7bf9136fc4a3" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Gravitational Force Between Two Objects"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "eea2af2f-8951-5c22-ac52-89e41da691d7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3.2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325738} {:answer/id #uuid "2b6537cd-d13e-5659-994b-00b98e4701d6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3.3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325739}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "eea2af2f-8951-5c22-ac52-89e41da691d7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3.2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325738} :answer-field/id #uuid "7426d33b-a42c-5f10-b77d-85adae98019f" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325737} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2d36bb84-283f-5622-a999-e65d4c463e7c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10^{-4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325741}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2d36bb84-283f-5622-a999-e65d4c463e7c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10^{-4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325741} :answer-field/id #uuid "2b466a9d-c4a8-5093-951a-5f94242e06ba" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325740}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "ed4a66f6-9dda-5b29-8f34-4f2d9fa1b7a7" :question/math-academy-id "q-336275" :question/problem "The centers of two objects of masses $(6.5\\times {10}^{5})\\,\\text{kg}$ and $(4.2\\times {10}^{6})\\,\\text{kg}$ are separated by a distance of $(7.5\\times {10}^{2})\\,\\text{m}.$ According to Newton's law of universal gravitation, what is the magnitude of the gravitational force between the two objects? Write your answer in scientific notation, rounding to **two significant figures** where appropriate. Assume the Newtonian constant of gravitation is $G=(6.67\\times {10}^{-11})\\,{\\text{m}}^{3}/(\\text{kg}\\cdot {\\text{s}}^{2}).$\n\nThe magnitude of the force between the two objects is\n$(${{field-1}}\n$\\times $\n{{field-2}}$)\\,\\text{N}.$" :question/worked-solution "Newton's law of universal gravitation states that the gravitational force attracting two objects is proportional to the product of the two masses and inversely proportional to the square of the distance between their centers of mass:\n\n$$\nF=G\\frac{{m}_{1}{m}_{2}}{{r}^{2}}\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- $F$ is the gravitational force between the objects, measured in Newtons $(\\text{N}),$\n\n- $G=(6.67\\times {10}^{-11})\\,{\\text{m}}^{3}/(\\text{kg}\\cdot {\\text{s}}^{2})$ is the Newtonian constant of gravitation,\n\n- ${m}_{1}$ and ${m}_{2}$ are the masses of the objects, measured in $\\text{kg},$ and\n\n- $r$ is the distance between their centers of mass, measured in $\\text{m}.$\n\nChoosing ${m}_{1}=(6.5\\times {10}^{5})\\,\\text{kg}$ and ${m}_{2}=(4.2\\times {10}^{6})\\,\\text{kg}$ and substituting in the other known values, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F & =(6.67\\times {10}^{-11})\\cdot \\frac{(6.5\\times {10}^{5})\\cdot (4.2\\times {10}^{6})}{(7.5\\times {10}^{2})^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{6.67\\cdot 6.5\\cdot 4.2}{(7.5)^{2}}\\times \\frac{{10}^{-11}\\cdot {10}^{5}\\cdot {10}^{6}}{({10}^{2})^{2}} \\\\  & =3.237\\,173…\\times {10}^{-4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, rounded to two significant figures, the magnitude of the gravitational force between the two objects is\n$(\\,3.2\\times {10}^{-4}\\,)\\,\\text{N}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186325742 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "61c3724b-7fda-5547-b0cb-7bf9136fc4a3" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Gravitational Force Between Two Objects"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e0d96b2c-e64e-5a25-be6b-12a05596eee7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1.98" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325744}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e0d96b2c-e64e-5a25-be6b-12a05596eee7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1.98" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325744} :answer-field/id #uuid "39774bd9-4fb1-571f-a975-9bdfbb6b9b60" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325743} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "74b85c08-0277-5308-8d73-877c87b1de78" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10^{20}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325746}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "74b85c08-0277-5308-8d73-877c87b1de78" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10^{20}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325746} :answer-field/id #uuid "047f66d7-e64d-5699-ae62-5537f5a472e0" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325745}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "6558c477-bf49-5669-953a-87da3d8bc427" :question/math-academy-id "q-336278" :question/problem "Earth has a mass of $(5.97\\times {10}^{24})\\,\\text{kg}$ and the Moon has a mass of $(7.35\\times {10}^{22})\\,\\text{kg}.$\nThe average distance between the centers of Earth and the Moon is $(3.84\\times {10}^{8})\\,\\text{m}.$ According to Newton's law of universal gravitation, what is the magnitude of the gravitational force between Earth and the Moon? Write your answer in scientific notation, rounding to **three significant figures** where appropriate. Assume the Newtonian constant of gravitation is $G=(6.67\\times {10}^{-11})\\,{\\text{m}}^{3}/(\\text{kg}\\cdot {\\text{s}}^{2}).$\n\nThe magnitude of the force between Earth and the Moon is\n$(${{field-1}}\n$\\times $\n{{field-2}}$)\\,\\text{N}.$" :question/worked-solution "Newton's law of universal gravitation states that the gravitational force attracting two objects is proportional to the product of the two masses and inversely proportional to the square of the distance between their centers of mass:\n\n$$\nF=G\\frac{{m}_{1}{m}_{2}}{{r}^{2}}\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- $F$ is the gravitational force between the objects, measured in Newtons $(\\text{N}),$\n\n- $G=(6.67\\times {10}^{-11})\\,{\\text{m}}^{3}/(\\text{kg}\\cdot {\\text{s}}^{2})$ is the Newtonian constant of gravitation,\n\n- ${m}_{1}$ and ${m}_{2}$ are the masses of the objects, measured in $\\text{kg},$ and\n\n- $r$ is the distance between their centers of mass, measured in $\\text{m}.$\n\nLet ${m}_{1}$ be the mass of Earth and ${m}_{2}$ be the mass of the Moon. Then, substituting in the known values, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F & =(6.67\\times {10}^{-11})\\cdot \\frac{(5.97\\times {10}^{24})\\cdot (7.35\\times {10}^{22})}{(3.84\\times {10}^{8})^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{6.67\\cdot 5.97\\cdot 7.35}{(3.84)^{2}}\\times \\frac{{10}^{-11}\\cdot {10}^{24}\\cdot {10}^{22}}{({10}^{8})^{2}} \\\\  & =(19.848\\,37…)\\times {10}^{19} \\\\  & =(1.984\\,837…)\\times {10}^{20}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, rounded to three significant figures, the gravitational force between Earth and the Moon is\n$(\\,1.98\\times {10}^{20}\\,)\\,\\text{N}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186325747 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "61c3724b-7fda-5547-b0cb-7bf9136fc4a3" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Gravitational Force Between Two Objects"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "bd94871e-7423-50ff-8e08-bf3a64280a68" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3.68" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325749} {:answer/id #uuid "802d4680-3e12-5b99-be80-bdeb62b59ecc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3.69" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325750}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "802d4680-3e12-5b99-be80-bdeb62b59ecc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3.69" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325750} :answer-field/id #uuid "04dccbb1-bbb8-5793-a497-51df0373e4ba" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325748} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "804f9fea-1733-5b65-8d9f-46104961638d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10^{22}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325752}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "804f9fea-1733-5b65-8d9f-46104961638d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10^{22}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325752} :answer-field/id #uuid "a22a327b-9cbc-5eb5-baa5-1d306b4f2323" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325751}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "793ce9bc-ad18-5544-94cd-bbb8ccdf129e" :question/math-academy-id "q-336280" :question/problem "The Sun has a mass of $(1.99\\times {10}^{30})\\,\\text{kg}$ and Saturn has a mass of $(5.68\\times {10}^{26})\\,\\text{kg}.$\nThe average distance between the centers of the Sun and Saturn is $(1.43\\times {10}^{12})\\,\\text{m}.$ According to Newton's law of universal gravitation, what is the magnitude of the gravitational force between the Sun and Saturn? Write your answer in scientific notation, rounding to **three significant figures** where appropriate. Assume the Newtonian constant of gravitation is $G=(6.67\\times {10}^{-11})\\,{\\text{m}}^{3}/(\\text{kg}\\cdot {\\text{s}}^{2}).$\n\nThe magnitude of the force between the Sun and Saturn is\n$(${{field-1}}\n$\\times $\n{{field-2}}$)\\,\\text{N}.$" :question/worked-solution "Newton's law of universal gravitation states that the gravitational force attracting two objects is proportional to the product of the two masses and inversely proportional to the square of the distance between their centers of mass:\n\n$$\nF=G\\frac{{m}_{1}{m}_{2}}{{r}^{2}}\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- $F$ is the gravitational force between the objects, measured in Newtons $(\\text{N}),$\n\n- $G=(6.67\\times {10}^{-11})\\,{\\text{m}}^{3}/(\\text{kg}\\cdot {\\text{s}}^{2})$ is the Newtonian constant of gravitation,\n\n- ${m}_{1}$ and ${m}_{2}$ are the masses of the objects, measured in $\\text{kg},$ and\n\n- $r$ is the distance between their centers of mass, measured in $\\text{m}.$\n\nLet ${m}_{1}$ be the mass of the Sun and ${m}_{2}$ be the mass of Saturn. Then, substituting in the known values, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F & =(6.67\\times {10}^{-11})\\cdot \\frac{(1.99\\times {10}^{30})\\cdot (5.68\\times {10}^{26})}{(1.43\\times {10}^{12})^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{6.67\\cdot 1.99\\cdot 5.68}{(1.43)^{2}}\\times \\frac{{10}^{-11}\\cdot {10}^{30}\\cdot {10}^{26}}{({10}^{12})^{2}} \\\\  & =(36.868\\,47…)\\times {10}^{21} \\\\  & =(3.686\\,847…)\\times {10}^{22}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, rounded to three significant figures, the gravitational force between the Sun and Saturn is\n$(\\,3.69\\times {10}^{22}\\,)\\,\\text{N}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186325753 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "cd5da040-4fa3-5aeb-a98f-2a2ba03f5e16" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Mass of an Object"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "84906d65-dc18-5ee8-9f92-daa65a9fba3e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9.1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325755}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "84906d65-dc18-5ee8-9f92-daa65a9fba3e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9.1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325755} :answer-field/id #uuid "e992fe4d-3ca3-5f48-ab32-57e0a66ac82e" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325754} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "55421f58-c82f-557a-88ba-c920a1edcb20" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10^{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325757}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "55421f58-c82f-557a-88ba-c920a1edcb20" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10^{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325757} :answer-field/id #uuid "aeb84aae-9340-51ef-aa0c-3148576a5c43" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325756}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "144b1af7-298a-5c58-a957-910148842530" :question/math-academy-id "q-336284" :question/problem "The centers of two objects are separated by a distance of $(7.2\\times {10}^{2})\\,\\text{m}$ and the magnitude of the gravitational force between them is $(5.6\\times {10}^{-4})\\,\\text{N}.$ If one object has mass $(4.8\\times {10}^{6})\\,\\text{kg},$ then according to Newton's law of universal gravitation, what is the mass of the other object? Write your answer in scientific notation, rounding to **two significant figures** where appropriate. Assume the Newtonian constant of gravitation is $G=(6.67\\times {10}^{-11})\\,{\\text{m}}^{3}/(\\text{kg}\\cdot {\\text{s}}^{2}).$\n\nThe mass of the other object is\n$(${{field-1}}\n$\\times $\n{{field-2}}$)\\,\\text{kg}.$" :question/worked-solution "Newton's law of universal gravitation states that the gravitational force attracting two objects is proportional to the product of the two masses and inversely proportional to the square of the distance between their centers of mass:\n\n$$\nF=G\\frac{{m}_{1}{m}_{2}}{{r}^{2}}\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- $F$ is the gravitational force between the objects, measured in Newtons $(\\text{N}),$\n\n- $G=(6.67\\times {10}^{-11})\\,{\\text{m}}^{3}/(\\text{kg}\\cdot {\\text{s}}^{2})$ is the Newtonian constant of gravitation,\n\n- ${m}_{1}$ and ${m}_{2}$ are the masses of the objects, measured in $\\text{kg},$ and\n\n- $r$ is the distance between their centers of mass, measured in $\\text{m}.$\n\nLet ${m}_{2}$ be the unknown mass.\nFirst, let's rearrange the formula to make ${m}_{2}$ the subject:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F & =G\\frac{{m}_{1}{m}_{2}}{{r}^{2}} \\\\ F{r}^{2} & =G{m}_{1}{m}_{2} \\\\ {m}_{2} & =\\frac{F{r}^{2}}{G{m}_{1}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, substituting in the known values, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{m}_{2} & =\\frac{(5.6\\times {10}^{-4})\\cdot (7.2\\times {10}^{2})^{2}}{(6.67\\times {10}^{-11})\\cdot (4.8\\times {10}^{6})} \\\\  & =\\frac{5.6\\cdot (7.2)^{2}}{6.67\\cdot 4.8}\\times \\frac{{10}^{-4}\\cdot ({10}^{2})^{2}}{{10}^{-11}\\cdot {10}^{6}} \\\\  & =(9.067…)\\times {10}^{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, rounded to two significant figures, the mass of the other object is\n$(\\,9.1\\times {10}^{5}\\,)\\,\\text{kg}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186325758 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "cd5da040-4fa3-5aeb-a98f-2a2ba03f5e16" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Mass of an Object"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3e572fa3-6dc0-5123-b5e3-fabd31c1b212" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7.5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325760}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3e572fa3-6dc0-5123-b5e3-fabd31c1b212" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7.5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325760} :answer-field/id #uuid "4b2131c7-b4df-536b-8c20-cce186db83b0" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325759} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b661ab6c-4b0d-5e0d-b713-e271da69d111" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10^{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325762}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b661ab6c-4b0d-5e0d-b713-e271da69d111" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10^{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325762} :answer-field/id #uuid "58bcc565-e611-5910-9f31-b92ef8fb5f85" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325761}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "eb51dbda-1d0c-5aa7-bf52-2dc4dd1466f6" :question/math-academy-id "q-336288" :question/problem "The centers of two objects are separated by a distance of $(5.0\\times {10}^{2})\\,\\text{m}$ and the magnitude of the gravitational force between them is $(7.2\\times {10}^{-5})\\,\\text{N}.$ If one object has mass $(3.6\\times {10}^{6})\\,\\text{kg},$ then according to Newton's law of universal gravitation, what is the mass of the other object? Write your answer in scientific notation, rounding to **two significant figures** where appropriate. Assume the Newtonian constant of gravitation is $G=(6.67\\times {10}^{-11})\\,{\\text{m}}^{3}/(\\text{kg}\\cdot {\\text{s}}^{2}).$\n\nThe mass of the other object is\n$(${{field-1}}\n$\\times $\n{{field-2}}$)\\,\\text{kg}.$" :question/worked-solution "Newton's law of universal gravitation states that the gravitational force attracting two objects is proportional to the product of the two masses and inversely proportional to the square of the distance between their centers of mass:\n\n$$\nF=G\\frac{{m}_{1}{m}_{2}}{{r}^{2}}\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- $F$ is the gravitational force between the objects, measured in Newtons $(\\text{N}),$\n\n- $G=(6.67\\times {10}^{-11})\\,{\\text{m}}^{3}/(\\text{kg}\\cdot {\\text{s}}^{2})$ is the Newtonian constant of gravitation,\n\n- ${m}_{1}$ and ${m}_{2}$ are the masses of the objects, measured in $\\text{kg},$ and\n\n- $r$ is the distance between their centers of mass, measured in $\\text{m}.$\n\nLet ${m}_{2}$ be the unknown mass.\nFirst, let's rearrange the formula to make ${m}_{2}$ the subject:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F & =G\\frac{{m}_{1}{m}_{2}}{{r}^{2}} \\\\ F{r}^{2} & =G{m}_{1}{m}_{2} \\\\ {m}_{2} & =\\frac{F{r}^{2}}{G{m}_{1}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, substituting in the known values, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{m}_{2} & =\\frac{(7.2\\times {10}^{-5})\\cdot (5.0\\times {10}^{2})^{2}}{(6.67\\times {10}^{-11})\\cdot (3.6\\times {10}^{6})} \\\\  & =\\frac{7.2\\cdot (5.0)^{2}}{6.67\\cdot 3.6}\\times \\frac{{10}^{-5}\\cdot ({10}^{2})^{2}}{{10}^{-11}\\cdot {10}^{6}} \\\\  & =(7.496…)\\times {10}^{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, rounded to two significant figures, the mass of the other object is\n$(\\,7.5\\times {10}^{4}\\,)\\,\\text{kg}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186325763 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "cd5da040-4fa3-5aeb-a98f-2a2ba03f5e16" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Mass of an Object"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f0f7b062-a88e-5452-99c1-6836f1b33a71" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1.4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325765} {:answer/id #uuid "56b52f70-a076-54ca-8e37-ba020d351409" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1.3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325766}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "56b52f70-a076-54ca-8e37-ba020d351409" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1.3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325766} :answer-field/id #uuid "70320651-2df9-5e33-a58a-f1678acfd6ad" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325764} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b3ace46e-2ad8-5cbb-ba52-79f9402d0207" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10^{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325768}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b3ace46e-2ad8-5cbb-ba52-79f9402d0207" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10^{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325768} :answer-field/id #uuid "39934434-4720-5294-bef2-33873420dc00" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325767}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "f9100a36-238f-5f12-b050-fca2cc3f1c62" :question/math-academy-id "q-336291" :question/problem "The centers of two objects are separated by a distance of $(5.0\\times {10}^{3})\\,\\text{m}$ and the magnitude of the gravitational force between them is $(7.0\\times {10}^{-6})\\,\\text{N}.$ If one object has mass $(2.0\\times {10}^{5})\\,\\text{kg},$ then according to Newton's law of universal gravitation, what is the mass of the other object? Write your answer in scientific notation, rounding to **two significant figures** where appropriate. Assume the Newtonian constant of gravitation is $G=(6.67\\times {10}^{-11})\\,{\\text{m}}^{3}/(\\text{kg}\\cdot {\\text{s}}^{2}).$\n\nThe mass of the other object is\n$(${{field-1}}\n$\\times $\n{{field-2}}$)\\,\\text{kg}.$" :question/worked-solution "Newton's law of universal gravitation states that the gravitational force attracting two objects is proportional to the product of the two masses and inversely proportional to the square of the distance between their centers of mass:\n\n$$\nF=G\\frac{{m}_{1}{m}_{2}}{{r}^{2}}\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- $F$ is the gravitational force between the objects, measured in Newtons $(\\text{N}),$\n\n- $G=(6.67\\times {10}^{-11})\\,{\\text{m}}^{3}/(\\text{kg}\\cdot {\\text{s}}^{2})$ is the Newtonian constant of gravitation,\n\n- ${m}_{1}$ and ${m}_{2}$ are the masses of the objects, measured in $\\text{kg},$ and\n\n- $r$ is the distance between their centers of mass, measured in $\\text{m}.$\n\nLet ${m}_{2}$ be the unknown mass.\nFirst, let's rearrange the formula to make ${m}_{2}$ the subject:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F & =G\\frac{{m}_{1}{m}_{2}}{{r}^{2}} \\\\ F{r}^{2} & =G{m}_{1}{m}_{2} \\\\ {m}_{2} & =\\frac{F{r}^{2}}{G{m}_{1}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, substituting in the known values, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{m}_{2} & =\\frac{(7.0\\times {10}^{-6})\\cdot (5.0\\times {10}^{3})^{2}}{(6.67\\times {10}^{-11})\\cdot (2.0\\times {10}^{5})} \\\\  & =\\frac{7.0\\cdot (5.0)^{2}}{6.67\\cdot 2.0}\\times \\frac{{10}^{-6}\\cdot ({10}^{3})^{2}}{{10}^{-11}\\cdot {10}^{5}} \\\\  & =(13.118…)\\times {10}^{6} \\\\  & =(1.3118…)\\times {10}^{7}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, rounded to two significant figures, the mass of the other object is\n$(\\,1.3\\times {10}^{7}\\,)\\,\\text{kg}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186325769 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "cd5da040-4fa3-5aeb-a98f-2a2ba03f5e16" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Mass of an Object"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4975eb76-be7f-5e01-8177-12ba3214a4f7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6.42" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325771} {:answer/id #uuid "6cb068df-33ce-5d54-acc3-0740c68cc901" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6.43" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325772}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4975eb76-be7f-5e01-8177-12ba3214a4f7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6.42" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325771} :answer-field/id #uuid "f08a0d75-84fe-5d32-adf6-e90bb45a33cd" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325770} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ecdd5219-8a1c-5ec4-a19b-ec34888f4ed3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10^{23}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325774}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ecdd5219-8a1c-5ec4-a19b-ec34888f4ed3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10^{23}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325774} :answer-field/id #uuid "648e1227-f8f3-5af6-8552-cc4e390aef1f" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325773}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "a55596a7-85a7-5686-925d-481e636872b1" :question/math-academy-id "q-336295" :question/problem "The average distance between the centers of the Sun and Mars is $(2.28\\times {10}^{11})\\,\\text{m}$ and the magnitude of the gravitational force between them is $(1.64\\times {10}^{21})\\,\\text{N}.$ Given that the mass of the Sun is $(1.99\\times {10}^{30})\\,\\text{kg},$ then according to Newton's law of universal gravitation, what is the mass of Mars? Write your answer in scientific notation, rounding to **three significant figures** where appropriate. Assume the Newtonian constant of gravitation is $G=(6.67\\times {10}^{-11})\\,{\\text{m}}^{3}/(\\text{kg}\\cdot {\\text{s}}^{2}).$\n\nThe mass of Mars is\n$(${{field-1}}\n$\\times $\n{{field-2}}$)\\,\\text{kg}.$" :question/worked-solution "Newton's law of universal gravitation states that the gravitational force attracting two objects is proportional to the product of the two masses and inversely proportional to the square of the distance between their centers of mass:\n\n$$\nF=G\\frac{{m}_{1}{m}_{2}}{{r}^{2}}\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- $F$ is the gravitational force between the objects, measured in Newtons $(\\text{N}),$\n\n- $G=(6.67\\times {10}^{-11})\\,{\\text{m}}^{3}/(\\text{kg}\\cdot {\\text{s}}^{2})$ is the Newtonian constant of gravitation,\n\n- ${m}_{1}$ and ${m}_{2}$ are the masses of the objects, measured in $\\text{kg},$ and\n\n- $r$ is the distance between their centers of mass, measured in $\\text{m}.$\n\nLet ${m}_{1}$ be the mass of the Sun and ${m}_{2}$ be the mass of Mars.\nFirst, let's rearrange the formula to make ${m}_{2}$ the subject:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F & =G\\frac{{m}_{1}{m}_{2}}{{r}^{2}} \\\\ F{r}^{2} & =G{m}_{1}{m}_{2} \\\\ {m}_{2} & =\\frac{F{r}^{2}}{G{m}_{1}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, substituting in the known values, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{m}_{2} & =\\frac{(1.64\\times {10}^{21})\\cdot (2.28\\times {10}^{11})^{2}}{(6.67\\times {10}^{-11})\\cdot (1.99\\times {10}^{30})} \\\\  & =\\frac{1.64\\cdot (2.28)^{2}}{6.67\\cdot 1.99}\\times \\frac{{10}^{21}\\cdot ({10}^{11})^{2}}{{10}^{-11}\\cdot {10}^{30}} \\\\  & =(0.642\\,29…)\\times {10}^{24} \\\\  & =(6.4229…)\\times {10}^{23}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, rounded to three significant figures, the mass of Mars is\n$(\\,6.42\\times {10}^{23}\\,)\\,\\text{kg}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186325775 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "cd5da040-4fa3-5aeb-a98f-2a2ba03f5e16" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Mass of an Object"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "582046b8-da4d-5542-997a-4b789391d84e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4.87" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325777}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "582046b8-da4d-5542-997a-4b789391d84e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4.87" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325777} :answer-field/id #uuid "7114e7c0-5463-5f44-ac4d-246c637b250c" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325776} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ed9a1d8f-b62f-5d78-9596-3386b3ab583b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10^{24}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325779}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ed9a1d8f-b62f-5d78-9596-3386b3ab583b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10^{24}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325779} :answer-field/id #uuid "837c34a0-cf89-530f-b49f-02c7760e6e78" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325778}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "dfb76479-723d-50b6-859a-82fc643abe38" :question/math-academy-id "q-336297" :question/problem "The average distance between the centers of the Sun and Venus is $(1.08\\times {10}^{11})\\,\\text{m}$ and the magnitude of the gravitational force between them is $(5.54\\times {10}^{22})\\,\\text{N}.$ Given that the mass of the Sun is $(1.99\\times {10}^{30})\\,\\text{kg},$ then according to Newton's law of universal gravitation, what is the mass of Venus? Write your answer in scientific notation, rounding to **three significant figures** where appropriate. Assume the Newtonian constant of gravitation is $G=(6.67\\times {10}^{-11})\\,{\\text{m}}^{3}/(\\text{kg}\\cdot {\\text{s}}^{2}).$\n\nThe mass of Venus is\n$(${{field-1}}\n$\\times $\n{{field-2}}$)\\,\\text{kg}.$" :question/worked-solution "Newton's law of universal gravitation states that the gravitational force attracting two objects is proportional to the product of the two masses and inversely proportional to the square of the distance between their centers of mass:\n\n$$\nF=G\\frac{{m}_{1}{m}_{2}}{{r}^{2}}\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- $F$ is the gravitational force between the objects, measured in Newtons $(\\text{N}),$\n\n- $G=(6.67\\times {10}^{-11})\\,{\\text{m}}^{3}/(\\text{kg}\\cdot {\\text{s}}^{2})$ is the Newtonian constant of gravitation,\n\n- ${m}_{1}$ and ${m}_{2}$ are the masses of the objects, measured in $\\text{kg},$ and\n\n- $r$ is the distance between their centers of mass, measured in $\\text{m}.$\n\nLet ${m}_{1}$ be the mass of the Sun and ${m}_{2}$ be the mass of Venus.\nFirst, let's rearrange the formula to make ${m}_{2}$ the subject:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F & =G\\frac{{m}_{1}{m}_{2}}{{r}^{2}} \\\\ F{r}^{2} & =G{m}_{1}{m}_{2} \\\\ {m}_{2} & =\\frac{F{r}^{2}}{G{m}_{1}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, substituting in the known values, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{m}_{2} & =\\frac{(5.54\\times {10}^{22})\\cdot (1.08\\times {10}^{11})^{2}}{(6.67\\times {10}^{-11})\\cdot (1.99\\times {10}^{30})} \\\\  & =\\frac{5.54\\cdot (1.08)^{2}}{6.67\\cdot 1.99}\\times \\frac{{10}^{22}\\cdot ({10}^{11})^{2}}{{10}^{-11}\\cdot {10}^{30}} \\\\  & =(0.486\\,83…)\\times {10}^{25} \\\\  & =(4.8683…)\\times {10}^{24}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, rounded to three significant figures, the mass of Venus is\n$(\\,4.87\\times {10}^{24}\\,)\\,\\text{kg}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186325780 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "11810d4b-610c-5dac-8fe7-abf9391c2aa7" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Distance Between Two Objects"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ce030b5d-1ca7-5c7f-adc0-3289398ca9ee" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3.9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325782}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ce030b5d-1ca7-5c7f-adc0-3289398ca9ee" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3.9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325782} :answer-field/id #uuid "1f3ec8a7-99fb-5682-9b22-f470d925a30d" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325781} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5f1e812b-ea8a-51ab-9427-33db6f5baafb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10^{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325784}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5f1e812b-ea8a-51ab-9427-33db6f5baafb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10^{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325784} :answer-field/id #uuid "be61949c-4a13-57cd-b2f9-4ace19f14745" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325783}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "e1a020c4-8b58-5a72-ba4b-09419cc286c1" :question/math-academy-id "q-336302" :question/problem "The gravitational force between two objects, of masses $(3.1\\times {10}^{6})\\,\\text{kg}$ and $(7.4\\times {10}^{5})\\,\\text{kg},$ has a magnitude of $(1.0\\times {10}^{-5})\\,\\text{N}.$\nAccording to Newton's law of universal gravitation, what is the distance between the centers of mass of the two objects? Write your answer in scientific notation, rounding to **two significant figures** where appropriate. Assume the Newtonian constant of gravitation is $G=(6.67\\times {10}^{-11})\\,{\\text{m}}^{3}/(\\text{kg}\\cdot {\\text{s}}^{2}).$\n\nThe distance between the two objects is\n$(${{field-1}}\n$\\times $\n{{field-2}}$)\\,\\text{m}.$" :question/worked-solution "Newton's law of universal gravitation states that the gravitational force attracting two objects is proportional to the product of the two masses and inversely proportional to the square of the distance between their centers of mass:\n\n$$\nF=G\\frac{{m}_{1}{m}_{2}}{{r}^{2}}\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- $F$ is the gravitational force between the objects, measured in Newtons $(\\text{N}),$\n\n- $G=(6.67\\times {10}^{-11})\\,{\\text{m}}^{3}/(\\text{kg}\\cdot {\\text{s}}^{2})$ is the Newtonian constant of gravitation,\n\n- ${m}_{1}$ and ${m}_{2}$ are the masses of the objects, measured in $\\text{kg},$ and\n\n- $r$ is the distance between their centers of mass, measured in $\\text{m}.$\n\nFirst, let's rearrange the formula to make $r$ the subject:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F & =G\\frac{{m}_{1}{m}_{2}}{{r}^{2}} \\\\ {r}^{2} & =G\\frac{{m}_{1}{m}_{2}}{F} \\\\ r & =\\sqrt{G\\frac{{m}_{1}{m}_{2}}{F}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nChoosing ${m}_{1}=(3.1\\times {10}^{6})\\,\\text{kg}$ and ${m}_{2}=(7.4\\times {10}^{5})\\,\\text{kg}$ and substituting in the other known values, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{2} & =(6.67\\times {10}^{-11})\\cdot \\frac{(3.1\\times {10}^{6})\\cdot (7.4\\times {10}^{5})}{1.0\\times {10}^{-5}} \\\\  & =\\frac{6.67\\cdot 3.1\\cdot 7.4}{1.0}\\times \\frac{{10}^{-11}\\cdot {10}^{6}\\cdot {10}^{5}}{{10}^{-5}} \\\\  & =153.0098\\times {10}^{5} \\\\  & =15.30098\\times {10}^{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, taking the square root, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =\\sqrt{15.30098\\times {10}^{6}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{15.30098}\\times \\sqrt{{10}^{6}} \\\\  & =(3.9116…)\\times {10}^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, rounded to two significant figures, the distance between the two objects is\n$(\\,3.9\\times {10}^{3}\\,)\\,\\text{m}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186325785 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "11810d4b-610c-5dac-8fe7-abf9391c2aa7" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Distance Between Two Objects"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "984a3d90-a1af-5611-85f2-61cfd6c6a0d4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2.0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325787} {:answer/id #uuid "b6fe00f5-52c3-55ee-a638-bafc7a5c4ec9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2.1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325788}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b6fe00f5-52c3-55ee-a638-bafc7a5c4ec9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2.1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325788} :answer-field/id #uuid "5403e1b5-4c7c-512f-a256-263fef324d87" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325786} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a7492222-4751-5959-8b61-b6194b874b40" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10^{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325790}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a7492222-4751-5959-8b61-b6194b874b40" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10^{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325790} :answer-field/id #uuid "8fda78b0-f98b-52e1-be9b-5d3c9af32ad2" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325789}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "e6a4d70b-6fe7-5e47-969b-79985cebce49" :question/math-academy-id "q-336304" :question/problem "The gravitational force between two objects, of masses $(2.7\\times {10}^{7})\\,\\text{kg}$ and $(1.8\\times {10}^{6})\\,\\text{kg},$ has a magnitude of $(7.5\\times {10}^{-6})\\,\\text{N}.$\nAccording to Newton's law of universal gravitation, what is the distance between the centers of mass of the two objects? Write your answer in scientific notation, rounding to **two significant figures** where appropriate. Assume the Newtonian constant of gravitation is $G=(6.67\\times {10}^{-11})\\,{\\text{m}}^{3}/(\\text{kg}\\cdot {\\text{s}}^{2}).$\n\nThe distance between the two objects is\n$(${{field-1}}\n$\\times $\n{{field-2}}$)\\,\\text{m}.$" :question/worked-solution "Newton's law of universal gravitation states that the gravitational force attracting two objects is proportional to the product of the two masses and inversely proportional to the square of the distance between their centers of mass:\n\n$$\nF=G\\frac{{m}_{1}{m}_{2}}{{r}^{2}}\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- $F$ is the gravitational force between the objects, measured in Newtons $(\\text{N}),$\n\n- $G=(6.67\\times {10}^{-11})\\,{\\text{m}}^{3}/(\\text{kg}\\cdot {\\text{s}}^{2})$ is the Newtonian constant of gravitation,\n\n- ${m}_{1}$ and ${m}_{2}$ are the masses of the objects, measured in $\\text{kg},$ and\n\n- $r$ is the distance between their centers of mass, measured in $\\text{m}.$\n\nFirst, let's rearrange the formula to make $r$ the subject:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F & =G\\frac{{m}_{1}{m}_{2}}{{r}^{2}} \\\\ {r}^{2} & =G\\frac{{m}_{1}{m}_{2}}{F} \\\\ r & =\\sqrt{G\\frac{{m}_{1}{m}_{2}}{F}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nChoosing ${m}_{1}=(2.7\\times {10}^{7})\\,\\text{kg}$ and ${m}_{2}=(1.8\\times {10}^{6})\\,\\text{kg}$ and substituting in the other known values, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{2} & =(6.67\\times {10}^{-11})\\cdot \\frac{(2.7\\times {10}^{7})\\cdot (1.8\\times {10}^{6})}{7.5\\times {10}^{-6}} \\\\  & =\\frac{6.67\\cdot 2.7\\cdot 1.8}{7.5}\\times \\frac{{10}^{-11}\\cdot {10}^{7}\\cdot {10}^{6}}{{10}^{-6}} \\\\  & =4.32216\\times {10}^{8}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, taking the square root, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =\\sqrt{4.32216\\times {10}^{8}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4.32216}\\times \\sqrt{{10}^{8}} \\\\  & =(2.078…)\\times {10}^{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, rounded to two significant figures, the distance between the two objects is\n$(\\,2.1\\times {10}^{4}\\,)\\,\\text{m}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186325791 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "11810d4b-610c-5dac-8fe7-abf9391c2aa7" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Distance Between Two Objects"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5355f7c1-2512-598f-bb82-a3b4ea31bfad" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1.28" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325793}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5355f7c1-2512-598f-bb82-a3b4ea31bfad" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1.28" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325793} :answer-field/id #uuid "ef3cbb9d-118d-562b-9e59-337532a06297" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325792} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "accb5ab6-6759-50e6-a958-f51796b85700" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10^{12}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325795}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "accb5ab6-6759-50e6-a958-f51796b85700" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10^{12}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325795} :answer-field/id #uuid "16204f78-4876-59d5-9c11-60037e9b9f74" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325794}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "78649320-4ab4-5f44-aca4-617ff44ab02f" :question/math-academy-id "q-336306" :question/problem "Earth has a mass of $(5.97\\times {10}^{24})\\,\\text{kg}$ and Saturn has a mass of $(5.68\\times {10}^{26})\\,\\text{kg}.$ The magnitude of the gravitational force between them is $(1.38\\times {10}^{17})\\,\\text{N}.$ According to Newton's law of universal gravitation, what is the distance between the centers of mass of Earth and Saturn? Write your answer in scientific notation, rounding to **three significant figures** where appropriate. Assume the Newtonian constant of gravitation is $G=(6.67\\times {10}^{-11})\\,{\\text{m}}^{3}/(\\text{kg}\\cdot {\\text{s}}^{2}).$\n\nThe distance between Earth and Saturn is\n$(${{field-1}}\n$\\times $\n{{field-2}}$)\\,\\text{m}.$" :question/worked-solution "Newton's law of universal gravitation states that the gravitational force attracting two objects is proportional to the product of the two masses and inversely proportional to the square of the distance between their centers of mass:\n\n$$\nF=G\\frac{{m}_{1}{m}_{2}}{{r}^{2}}\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- $F$ is the gravitational force between the objects, measured in Newtons $(\\text{N}),$\n\n- $G=(6.67\\times {10}^{-11})\\,{\\text{m}}^{3}/(\\text{kg}\\cdot {\\text{s}}^{2})$ is the Newtonian constant of gravitation,\n\n- ${m}_{1}$ and ${m}_{2}$ are the masses of the objects, measured in $\\text{kg},$ and\n\n- $r$ is the distance between their centers of mass, measured in $\\text{m}.$\n\nFirst, let's rearrange the formula to make $r$ the subject:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F & =G\\frac{{m}_{1}{m}_{2}}{{r}^{2}} \\\\ {r}^{2} & =G\\frac{{m}_{1}{m}_{2}}{F} \\\\ r & =\\sqrt{G\\frac{{m}_{1}{m}_{2}}{F}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet ${m}_{1}$ be the mass of Earth and ${m}_{2}$ be the mass of Saturn. Then, substituting in the known values, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{2} & =(6.67\\times {10}^{-11})\\cdot \\frac{(5.97\\times {10}^{24})\\cdot (5.68\\times {10}^{26})}{1.38\\times {10}^{17}} \\\\  & =\\frac{6.67\\cdot 5.97\\cdot 5.68}{1.38}\\times \\frac{{10}^{-11}\\cdot {10}^{24}\\cdot {10}^{26}}{{10}^{17}} \\\\  & =(163.8964)\\times {10}^{22}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, taking the square root, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =\\sqrt{163.8964\\times {10}^{22}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{163.8964}\\times \\sqrt{{10}^{22}} \\\\  & =(12.802…)\\times {10}^{11} \\\\  & =(1.2802…)\\times {10}^{12}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, rounded to three significant figures, the distance between Earth and Saturn is\n$(\\,1.28\\times {10}^{12}\\,)\\,\\text{m}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186325796 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "11810d4b-610c-5dac-8fe7-abf9391c2aa7" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Distance Between Two Objects"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d5cdab77-c04b-576a-a31a-801152bb621e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4.50" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325798}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d5cdab77-c04b-576a-a31a-801152bb621e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4.50" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325798} :answer-field/id #uuid "67fb7e89-801c-5c2f-94fc-a1ce07d0d6bb" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325797} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a4cd47a8-f051-515b-afe1-62c53811b217" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10^{12}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325800}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a4cd47a8-f051-515b-afe1-62c53811b217" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10^{12}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325800} :answer-field/id #uuid "d56be2f8-32aa-5e41-afaa-bde89e5da718" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325799}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "c329b403-e518-5355-bde4-804348667835" :question/math-academy-id "q-336307" :question/problem "The Sun has a mass of $(1.99\\times {10}^{30})\\,\\text{kg}$ and Neptune has a mass of $(1.02\\times {10}^{26})\\,\\text{kg}.$ The magnitude of the gravitational force between them is $(6.69\\times {10}^{20})\\,\\text{N}.$ According to Newton's law of universal gravitation, what is the distance between the centers of mass of the Sun and Neptune? Write your answer in scientific notation, rounding to **three significant figures** where appropriate. Assume the Newtonian constant of gravitation is $G=(6.67\\times {10}^{-11})\\,{\\text{m}}^{3}/(\\text{kg}\\cdot {\\text{s}}^{2}).$\n\nThe distance between the Sun and Neptune is\n$(${{field-1}}\n$\\times $\n{{field-2}}$)\\,\\text{m}.$" :question/worked-solution "Newton's law of universal gravitation states that the gravitational force attracting two objects is proportional to the product of the two masses and inversely proportional to the square of the distance between their centers of mass:\n\n$$\nF=G\\frac{{m}_{1}{m}_{2}}{{r}^{2}}\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- $F$ is the gravitational force between the objects, measured in Newtons $(\\text{N}),$\n\n- $G=(6.67\\times {10}^{-11})\\,{\\text{m}}^{3}/(\\text{kg}\\cdot {\\text{s}}^{2})$ is the Newtonian constant of gravitation,\n\n- ${m}_{1}$ and ${m}_{2}$ are the masses of the objects, measured in $\\text{kg},$ and\n\n- $r$ is the distance between their centers of mass, measured in $\\text{m}.$\n\nFirst, let's rearrange the formula to make $r$ the subject:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F & =G\\frac{{m}_{1}{m}_{2}}{{r}^{2}} \\\\ {r}^{2} & =G\\frac{{m}_{1}{m}_{2}}{F} \\\\ r & =\\sqrt{G\\frac{{m}_{1}{m}_{2}}{F}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet ${m}_{1}$ be the mass of the Sun and ${m}_{2}$ be the mass of Neptune. Then, substituting in the known values, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{2} & =(6.67\\times {10}^{-11})\\cdot \\frac{(1.99\\times {10}^{30})\\cdot (1.02\\times {10}^{26})}{6.69\\times {10}^{20}} \\\\  & =\\frac{6.67\\cdot 1.99\\cdot 1.02}{6.69}\\times \\frac{{10}^{-11}\\cdot {10}^{30}\\cdot {10}^{26}}{{10}^{20}} \\\\  & =(2.0237…)\\times {10}^{25} \\\\  & =(20.237…)\\times {10}^{24}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, taking the square root, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =\\sqrt{20.237…\\times {10}^{24}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{20.237…}\\times \\sqrt{{10}^{24}} \\\\  & =(4.498…)\\times {10}^{12}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, rounded to three significant figures, the distance between the Sun and Neptune is\n$(\\,4.50\\times {10}^{12}\\,)\\,\\text{m}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186325801 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "11810d4b-610c-5dac-8fe7-abf9391c2aa7" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Distance Between Two Objects"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "23e1033c-ca86-5cd7-be1f-428c9045bfcf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1.08" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325803} {:answer/id #uuid "9a12c534-4d6b-5e5a-aca4-32bdb28ead5b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1.09" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325804}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "23e1033c-ca86-5cd7-be1f-428c9045bfcf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1.08" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325803} :answer-field/id #uuid "9ab133a5-9ca9-5e8c-9fc0-13efdf2b6d7c" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325802} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "485a523d-4ef9-50ae-a8c9-e3c8e71c4d8c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10^{11}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325806}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "485a523d-4ef9-50ae-a8c9-e3c8e71c4d8c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10^{11}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325806} :answer-field/id #uuid "e8700950-5f95-531f-90ed-56f72d572bb7" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325805}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "ecb2bf60-38e1-5522-90d8-8c4b6ea16fda" :question/math-academy-id "q-336314" :question/problem "The Sun has a mass of $(1.99\\times {10}^{30})\\,\\text{kg}$ and Venus has a mass of $(4.87\\times {10}^{24})\\,\\text{kg}.$ The magnitude of the gravitational force between them is $(5.54\\times {10}^{22})\\,\\text{N}.$ According to Newton's law of universal gravitation, what is the distance between the centers of mass of the Sun and Venus? Write your answer in scientific notation, rounding to **three significant figures** where appropriate. Assume the Newtonian constant of gravitation is $G=(6.67\\times {10}^{-11})\\,{\\text{m}}^{3}/(\\text{kg}\\cdot {\\text{s}}^{2}).$\n\nThe distance between the Sun and Venus is\n$(${{field-1}}\n$\\times $\n{{field-2}}$)\\,\\text{m}.$" :question/worked-solution "Newton's law of universal gravitation states that the gravitational force attracting two objects is proportional to the product of the two masses and inversely proportional to the square of the distance between their centers of mass:\n\n$$\nF=G\\frac{{m}_{1}{m}_{2}}{{r}^{2}}\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- $F$ is the gravitational force between the objects, measured in Newtons $(\\text{N}),$\n\n- $G=(6.67\\times {10}^{-11})\\,{\\text{m}}^{3}/(\\text{kg}\\cdot {\\text{s}}^{2})$ is the Newtonian constant of gravitation,\n\n- ${m}_{1}$ and ${m}_{2}$ are the masses of the objects, measured in $\\text{kg},$ and\n\n- $r$ is the distance between their centers of mass, measured in $\\text{m}.$\n\nFirst, let's rearrange the formula to make $r$ the subject:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F & =G\\frac{{m}_{1}{m}_{2}}{{r}^{2}} \\\\ {r}^{2} & =G\\frac{{m}_{1}{m}_{2}}{F} \\\\ r & =\\sqrt{G\\frac{{m}_{1}{m}_{2}}{F}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet ${m}_{1}$ be the mass of the Sun and ${m}_{2}$ be the mass of Venus. Then, substituting in the known values, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{2} & =(6.67\\times {10}^{-11})\\cdot \\frac{(1.99\\times {10}^{30})\\cdot (4.87\\times {10}^{24})}{5.54\\times {10}^{22}} \\\\  & =\\frac{6.67\\cdot 1.99\\cdot 4.87}{5.54}\\times \\frac{{10}^{-11}\\cdot {10}^{30}\\cdot {10}^{24}}{{10}^{22}} \\\\  & =(11.668…)\\times {10}^{21} \\\\  & =(1.1668…)\\times {10}^{22}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, taking the square root, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =\\sqrt{1.1668…\\times {10}^{22}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{1.1668…}\\times \\sqrt{{10}^{22}} \\\\  & =(1.0801…)\\times {10}^{11}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, rounded to three significant figures, the distance between the Sun and Venus is\n$(\\,1.08\\times {10}^{11}\\,)\\,\\text{m}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186325807 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "61c3724b-7fda-5547-b0cb-7bf9136fc4a3" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Gravitational Force Between Two Objects"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6678cb68-6c92-5007-a1d3-92e1bf77feeb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2.22" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325809}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6678cb68-6c92-5007-a1d3-92e1bf77feeb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2.22" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325809} :answer-field/id #uuid "105d386c-1b11-57ef-9112-24fd05725ef0" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325808} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "43dfcf04-0d7c-5086-a199-aab94e113cbd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10^{17}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325811}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "43dfcf04-0d7c-5086-a199-aab94e113cbd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10^{17}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325811} :answer-field/id #uuid "0f016b1d-48fa-5eb6-8470-e63c41e9bfd4" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325810}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "7ed17f4b-12c8-582c-8030-88ae53a4f3ef" :question/math-academy-id "q-336321" :question/problem "Uranus has a mass of $(8.68\\times {10}^{25})\\,\\text{kg}$ and Neptune has a mass of $(1.02\\times {10}^{26})\\,\\text{kg}.$\nThe average distance between the centers of Uranus and Neptune is $(1.63\\times {10}^{12})\\,\\text{m}.$ According to Newton's law of universal gravitation, what is the magnitude of the gravitational force between Uranus and Neptune? Write your answer in scientific notation, rounding to **three significant figures** where appropriate. Assume the Newtonian constant of gravitation is $G=(6.67\\times {10}^{-11})\\,{\\text{m}}^{3}/(\\text{kg}\\cdot {\\text{s}}^{2}).$\n\nThe magnitude of the force between Uranus and Neptune is\n$(${{field-1}}\n$\\times $\n{{field-2}}$)\\,\\text{N}.$" :question/worked-solution "Newton's law of universal gravitation states that the gravitational force attracting two objects is proportional to the product of the two masses and inversely proportional to the square of the distance between their centers of mass:\n\n$$\nF=G\\frac{{m}_{1}{m}_{2}}{{r}^{2}}\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- $F$ is the gravitational force between the objects, measured in Newtons $(\\text{N}),$\n\n- $G=(6.67\\times {10}^{-11})\\,{\\text{m}}^{3}/(\\text{kg}\\cdot {\\text{s}}^{2})$ is the Newtonian constant of gravitation,\n\n- ${m}_{1}$ and ${m}_{2}$ are the masses of the objects, measured in $\\text{kg},$ and\n\n- $r$ is the distance between their centers of mass, measured in $\\text{m}.$\n\nLet ${m}_{1}$ be the mass of Uranus and ${m}_{2}$ be the mass of Neptune. Then, substituting in the known values, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F & =(6.67\\times {10}^{-11})\\cdot \\frac{(8.68\\times {10}^{25})\\cdot (1.02\\times {10}^{26})}{(1.63\\times {10}^{12})^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{6.67\\cdot 8.68\\cdot 1.02}{(1.63)^{2}}\\times \\frac{{10}^{-11}\\cdot {10}^{25}\\cdot {10}^{26}}{({10}^{12})^{2}} \\\\  & =(22.226\\,47…)\\times {10}^{16} \\\\  & =(2.222\\,647…)\\times {10}^{17}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, rounded to three significant figures, the gravitational force between Uranus and Neptune is\n$(\\,2.22\\times {10}^{17}\\,)\\,\\text{N}.$"}]}
